{
  "slug": "gita-govinda",
  "title": "The Gita Govinda",
  "originalTitle": "गीतगोविन्द",
  "transliteration": "Gītagovinda",
  "altTitles": [
    "Gita Govinda",
    "Song of the Cowherd",
    "Gita Gobindam"
  ],
  "tradition": "hinduism",
  "school": "vaishnavism",
  "genre": "Poetry",
  "textType": "Lyric poem with songs",
  "category": "Poetry",
  "subcategory": "Devotional poetry",
  "originalLanguage": "sa",
  "dateStart": 1150,
  "dateEnd": 1200,
  "dateDisplay": "c. late 12th century CE",
  "authorshipStatus": "established",
  "author": "jayadeva",
  "attributionNote": "Written by Jayadeva, a poet of Bengal, who names himself in the poem's verses and whose verses carry the musical modes and rythms to which they were sung.",
  "description": "The Gita Govinda is a poem of twelve cantos on Krishna and Radha: the joy of their meeting, Radha's grief and jealousy at Krishna's absence, her friend's pleading, the moonlit night on the Yamuna, and the reconciliation in which the poem's devotional and erotic senses are inseparable. Each canto closes in a song marked with its melody and rhythm.",
  "historicalContext": "Singing the Gita Govinda became part of temple worship in Odisha and Bengal, where it was performed for centuries; its treatment of divine love shaped the Vaishnava poetry of the whole of eastern India and the devotional theology written around Radha.",
  "availabilityNote": "Complete: the twelve cantos of the Sanskrit text as GRETIL publishes it, in the transliteration GRETIL prints from its source edition, streamed from GRETIL at read time, one canto at a time.",
